EXT. RAILROAD CROSSING - BROKEN-DOWN SCHOOL BUS - DUSK

A yellow bus idles dead on the tracks. The hood is up. Crossing lights blink red, and a distant horn sounds.

JUNE, 16, in a letterman jacket, kneels at the engine with a flashlight in her teeth. RAYMOND, 17, hovers beside her, shirt untucked. DEVI, 15, stands on the bus steps with a phone held high, hunting for a signal.

RAYMOND
It's the alternator.

JUNE
(around the flashlight)
It's not the alternator.

DEVI
I have one bar. One. Don't move.

RAYMOND
How would you know?

JUNE
My dad's a mechanic.

DEVI
Quiet, I'm calling.

RAYMOND
He fixes lawnmowers.

JUNE
He fixes everything.

DEVI
Hello? Hello? Is this 911?

RAYMOND
Tell them the train.

DEVI
There's a train coming!

JUNE
How far?

RAYMOND
Two miles.

JUNE
Three.

DEVI
They're asking where we are.

RAYMOND
Say Route Nine.

JUNE
Say the crossing.

DEVI
Route Nine, at the crossing!

The horn sounds again, nearer. All three look east.

JUNE
Out. Everyone out.

RAYMOND
I'm out.

DEVI
I'm out.

They run into the weeds, hand in hand in hand, as the bus sits alone in the red light.
